API Reference.Entry Point & CLI Dispatch:
## `main`

```dart
Future<void> main(List<String> arguments) async {
  try {
    await runAsd(arguments);
  } on FileSystemException catch (e) {
    // sudo retry logic, see Sudo Handling
  } on AsdException catch (e) {
    stderr.writeln('Error: ${e.message}');
    exitCode = 1;
  } catch (e) {
    stderr.writeln('Error: $e');
    exitCode = 1;
  }
}
```

All top-level error handling lives here. There are exactly three catch tiers:

1. `FileSystemException` — may trigger a transparent `sudo` re-exec (see [Sudo Handling](/asd/sudo-handling/)); otherwise printed as `Error: <message>: <path>`.
2. `AsdException` — the app's own typed error for expected failures (bad args, missing package, checksum mismatch, etc). Printed as `Error: <message>` with no stack trace, since these are meant to be read by end users.
3. Anything else — printed as `Error: $e` as a last-resort catch-all.

## `runAsd`

Resolves the three pieces of runtime config (`sdkRoot`, `csvFile`, `csvUrl`) into an `AsdConfig`, then dispatches on `args[0]`:

```dart
class AsdConfig {
  final String sdkRoot;
  final String csvFile;
  final String csvUrl; // always https://maven.myket.ir/sdk-archives.csv
}
```

Dispatch is a plain `switch` over recognized flags (`--list`/`-l`, `--installed`/`-i`, `--remove`/`-rm`, `--fix`/`-f`, `--update`/`-up`, `--verify`/`-v`, `--help`/`-h`). Anything not matching a known flag and not starting with `-` falls through to `doInstall(config, cmd, ...)`, treating the first argument as a package name. Anything starting with `-` that isn't recognized throws `AsdException("Unknown option '$cmd'")` rather than being misinterpreted as a package.

## `AsdException`

```dart
class AsdException implements Exception {
  final String message;
  AsdException(this.message);
}
```

The single error type used throughout the codebase for any failure that should be shown to the user as a clean one-line message rather than a stack trace — CSV parse failures, missing packages, checksum mismatches, network errors, malformed archives, and so on.

API Reference.SHA-1 Implementation:
`asd` includes a from-scratch, dependency-free SHA-1 implementation (`_Sha1`) used to verify downloaded archives against the registry's checksum column, since pulling in `package:crypto` would violate the project's zero-dependency constraint.

```dart
Future<String> sha1OfFile(String path) async {
  final sha1 = _Sha1();
  await for (final chunk in File(path).openRead()) {
    sha1.add(chunk);
  }
  return sha1.digestHex();
}
```

## `_Sha1`

A textbook streaming SHA-1: a 64-byte block buffer, the standard five 32-bit working registers (`_h0`..`_h4` initialized to the FIPS 180 constants), and the usual 80-round compression function operating on a word-expanded 80-entry schedule per 512-bit block. `add()` can be called repeatedly with arbitrarily-sized chunks (it buffers partial blocks internally), which is what lets it hash a file incrementally as it streams off disk rather than loading the whole archive into memory.

`digestHex()` applies standard SHA-1 padding (a `0x80` byte, zero-padding to 56 bytes mod 64, then the 64-bit bit-length in big-endian) before producing the final 40-character lowercase hex digest.

This is used in exactly two places: verifying a freshly-downloaded archive during `doInstall` (unless `ASD_SKIP_CHECKSUM=1`), and re-verifying a cached archive's integrity during `--fix`'s diagnosis pass and `--verify`.

API Reference.ZIP Extraction Engine:
`asd` implements ZIP reading itself rather than shelling out to `unzip`, so it can preserve Unix permissions and symlinks (see [Executable Permissions](/asd/executable-permissions/)) that a naive extraction would drop.

## `extractZip`

```dart
Future<void> extractZip(String zipPath, String destDir)
```

1. Reads the last `22 + 65536` bytes of the file (or the whole file if smaller) and scans backward for the End Of Central Directory signature (`0x06054b50`).
2. If the entry count or central-directory offset fields read `0xFFFF`/`0xFFFFFFFF` (the ZIP64 sentinel values), follows the ZIP64 end-of-central-directory locator (`0x07064b50`) to read the real 64-bit values instead — this is what lets `asd` handle archives larger than 4 GiB or with more than 65,535 entries.
3. Reads the full central directory and parses each entry (`0x02014b50` signature) into a `_ZipEntry`, including a ZIP64 extra-field parse (header ID `0x0001`) for any entry whose size or offset fields overflowed into the 32-bit sentinel.
4. Extracts every entry via `_extractEntry`.

## `_ZipEntry` and Unix metadata

```dart
class _ZipEntry {
  final String name;
  final int compressionMethod;   // 0 = stored, 8 = deflate
  final int compressedSize;
  final int uncompressedSize;
  final int localHeaderOffset;
  final int externalAttributes;
  final int versionMadeBy;

  int? get unixMode { ... }      // top 16 bits of externalAttributes, only if made-by host OS == Unix (3)
  bool get isSymlink => (unixMode ?? 0) & 0xF000 == 0xA000;
}
```

## `_extractEntry`

For each entry: seeks to its local file header, validates the local header signature independently of the central directory's (defense against malformed archives), reads the compressed bytes, and either copies them as-is (`compressionMethod == 0`) or inflates them with `ZLibDecoder(raw: true)` (`compressionMethod == 8` — any other method throws `AsdException`, since `asd` doesn't implement, say, bzip2 or LZMA).

Path traversal is blocked explicitly: any entry name that's empty, absolute (starts with `/`), or contains `..` is silently skipped rather than written to disk.

Symlink entries are recreated with `Link.create()` using the "file content" (which for a symlink is the target path as UTF-8 text) — skipped entirely on Windows. Regular files get their Unix permission bits (`mode & 0x1FF`) reapplied via a `chmod` subprocess call after being written — the one place in `asd` that does shell out to an external binary, since Dart's standard library has no cross-platform way to set POSIX permission bits directly.

## `flattenSingleSubdir`

Many SDK archives from the mirror wrap their real contents in a single top-level directory (e.g. `ndk-linux/...`). After extraction, if `destDir` contains exactly one entry and it's a directory, `flattenSingleSubdir` moves everything up one level and removes the now-empty wrapper — so `asd`'s installed layout matches what `sdkmanager` would have produced.

API Reference.Installer & Embedding:
## The problem this solves

In 1.0.0, the installer was a thin executable that looked for an `asd` (or `asd.exe`) binary sitting in the same directory as itself and copied it into place. That meant every release had to ship two files that had to stay together, and the installer would fail with a clear error if `asd` wasn't found next to it.

2.0.0 replaces this with a single self-contained installer.

## `tool/embed_installer.dart`

A standalone build-time script (not part of the shipped `asd` tool):

```dart
void main(List<String> args) {
  // args: <asd-binary> <installer-source> <output-source>
  final bytes = File(binaryPath).readAsBytesSync();
  final base64Payload = base64.encode(bytes);
  final source = File(installerSourcePath).readAsStringSync();
  final replaced = source.replaceFirst('__ASD_BASE64_PLACEHOLDER__', base64Payload);
  File(outputSourcePath).writeAsStringSync(replaced);
}
```

It reads the already-compiled `asd` binary, base64-encodes its raw bytes, and substitutes that string into a literal placeholder (`__ASD_BASE64_PLACEHOLDER__`) inside a copy of `bin/installer.dart`'s source — writing the result to a new file rather than mutating `installer.dart` itself. That generated source is then compiled normally.

## `bin/installer.dart` changes

The embedded payload is decoded and written to disk instead of being copied from a neighboring file:

```dart
const String _embeddedAsdBase64 = '__ASD_BASE64_PLACEHOLDER__'; // replaced at build time

Future<void> runInstaller(List<String> args) async {
  if (_embeddedAsdBase64.startsWith('__ASD_BASE64_PLACEHOLDER')) {
    throw AsdInstallException(
      'This installer was compiled without an embedded asd binary. '
      'Build it via tool/embed_installer.dart instead of compiling bin/installer.dart directly.',
    );
  }
  final asdBytes = base64.decode(_embeddedAsdBase64);
  await File(destPath).writeAsBytes(asdBytes);
  // chmod 755 on non-Windows
}
```

The placeholder-detection check is the safety net for the case where someone compiles `bin/installer.dart` directly without running the embed step — rather than shipping a broken installer that writes a truncated or nonsense binary, it fails immediately with an explanatory error.

API Reference.Entry Point & CLI Dispatch:
```dart
Future<void> main(List<String> arguments) async {
  try {
    await runAsd(arguments);
  } on FileSystemException catch (e) {
    // sudo retry
  } on AsdException catch (e) {
    stderr.writeln('Error: ${e.message}');
    exitCode = 1;
  } catch (e) {
    stderr.writeln('Error: $e');
    exitCode = 1;
  }
}
```

This structure — three catch tiers (`FileSystemException` for sudo retry, `AsdException` for expected user-facing errors, catch-all for everything else) and a plain `switch` dispatch on `args[0]` in `runAsd` — is identical across all three published versions (1.0.0, 2.0.0, 3.0.0). Nothing here changed later.

API Reference.CSV Registry:
```dart
class PkgEntry {
  final String path, name, rev, url, checksum;
}

PkgEntry? findMatch(List<List<String>> rows, String name, String version)
```

`findMatch`'s logic — match `<name>;<version>` exactly, or `name` with a matching version column, or (with no version given) the first row whose path equals or starts with `<name>;` — is identical in every published version. `readCsvRows` and `ensureCsvAvailable` are likewise unchanged.

API Reference.HTTP & Download Engine:
## `downloadFile`

```dart
Future<void> downloadFile(String url, String destPath) async {
  final connections = int.tryParse(Platform.environment['ASD_CONNECTIONS'] ?? '') ?? 8;
  // HEAD request for size + range support
  if (totalSize != null && acceptsRanges && totalSize > 1024 * 1024 && connections > 1) {
    await _downloadRanged(client, uri, destPath, totalSize, connections);
  } else {
    await _downloadSingle(client, uri, destPath, totalSize);
  }
}
```

Two notable differences from 3.0.0:

- The multi-connection threshold is **1 MiB** here, not 4 MiB, and the connection count isn't scaled down for smaller files — with default settings, an 1.5 MiB file would open all 8 connections.
- **`ASD_CONNECTIONS` defaults to `8`**, not `16`.

## `_downloadSingle`

No resume, no retry:

```dart
Future<void> _downloadSingle(HttpClient client, Uri uri, String destPath, int? totalSize) async {
  final request = await client.getUrl(uri);
  final response = await request.close();
  if (response.statusCode != 200) throw AsdException('HTTP ${response.statusCode} for $uri');
  final sink = File(destPath).openWrite();
  await for (final chunk in response) {
    sink.add(chunk);
    received += chunk.length;
    _printProgress(received, totalSize);
  }
  await sink.close();
}
```

Any error propagates straight out of `downloadFile` as a failure; the caller (`doInstall`) has no retry loop either, so a dropped connection means running the install command again from scratch.

## `_downloadRanged` / `_downloadChunkToFile`

Splits the file into `connections` chunks, each downloaded once with no retry on failure — if any single chunk's HTTP request throws, `Future.wait` propagates that failure and the whole download fails, leaving the `.partN` files behind for the *next full attempt* to silently overwrite (they aren't reused, since 1.0.0's `_downloadChunkToFile` always opens each part file in write mode from the start, not append).

## `_printProgress`

A minimal helper printing `\rDownloading... 42.3% (128.4MB / 303.9MB)` on a plain 200ms timer — no `_Progress` class, no speed sampling, no ETA. Those were introduced in 3.0.0's `_Progress` class.

API Reference.ZIP Extraction Engine:
1.0.0's `extractZip` already includes full ZIP64 central-directory parsing (the End-Of-Central-Directory scan, the ZIP64 locator/EOCD fallback for archives with `0xFFFF`/`0xFFFFFFFF` sentinel values, and the ZIP64 extra-field parse for individual oversized entries) — this was **not** added later; it was present from the first release.

What 1.0.0's `_ZipEntry` does **not** carry is any Unix permission or symlink metadata:

```dart
class _ZipEntry {
  final String name;
  final int compressionMethod;
  final int compressedSize;
  final int uncompressedSize;
  final int localHeaderOffset;
  // no externalAttributes, no versionMadeBy
}
```

`_extractEntry` writes every non-directory entry as a plain file with default permissions and never creates a symlink — a ZIP containing a symlink entry (as many NDK archives do) would have that entry extracted as a regular file containing the link-target string as its content, not an actual symlink, and executables inside the archive would come out without their executable bit set. Preserving Unix permission bits and recreating symlinks (via a new `unixMode`/`isSymlink` getter reading `externalAttributes`/`versionMadeBy`) was added in 3.0.0 — see [Executable Permissions](/asd/executable-permissions/) in the current docs.
